            %p Denote the cube faces by F (Front), B (Back), U (Up), D (Down), L (Left), and R (Right) (this is Rubik’s cube Singmaster notation).
            %p a. Assume F and D are pullback squares. Then the following are equivalent:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  The total rectangle from
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo ′
                        %mo →
                        %mi Z
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W'\to Z'
                  to
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
                  is a pullback.
              %li
                %p T and B are pullback squares.
              %li
                %p T or B is a pullback square.
            %p
              b. Assume F, D, R are pullback squares (these are the faces sharing
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              as a common vertex). Then all three other faces B, T, L are also pullback squares if at least one of them is.
            %p a’. Assume T and B are pushout squares. Then the following are equivalent:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  The total rectangle from
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo ′
                        %mo →
                        %mi Z
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W'\to Z'
                  to
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
                  is a pushout.
              %li
                %p F and D are pushout squares.
              %li
                %p F or D is a pushout square.
            %p
              b’. Assume B, T, L are pushout squares (these are the faces sharing
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W'
              as a common vertex). Then all three other faces F, D, R are also pushout squares if at least one of them is.
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              We only show a and b (for a’ and b’ are their duals). Part a follows directly from Proposition
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GeneralPastingLaw"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W'
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
